## 9. CMS-Driven Content Architecture

### The Problem
Hardcoded content in JSX means every text change requires:
1. A developer to edit the source file
2. A commit, push, and deployment
3. 3–5 minute build cycle on Vercel

### The Solution: Backend CMS with React Query

#### Backend: Site Content Model
```python
# app/models/site_content.py
class SiteContent(db.Model):
    id = db.Column(db.Integer, primary_key=True)
    section = db.Column(db.String(100), unique=True, nullable=False, index=True)
    content = db.Column(db.JSON, default=dict)
    updated_at = db.Column(db.DateTime, default=datetime.utcnow, onupdate=datetime.utcnow)
```

Each `section` (e.g., `"hero"`, `"about_intro"`, `"services"`) stores its content as a flexible JSON blob.

#### Backend: REST API
```python
# GET /api/site-content/hero → Returns the hero block
# PUT /api/site-content/hero → Admin updates the hero block
```

#### Frontend: React Query Hook
```js
// hooks/useSiteContent.js
export function useContentBlock(section) {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: ['site-content', section],
    queryFn: async () => {
      const response = await api.get(`/api/site-content/${section}`);
      return response.data;
    },
    enabled: !!section,
  });
}
```

#### Frontend: Component Usage
```jsx
const Hero = () => {
  const { data: block, isLoading } = useContentBlock('hero');
  const headline = block?.content?.headline || "Default Headline";
  
  return (
    <h1>{isLoading ? <Skeleton /> : headline}</h1>
  );
};
```

### Content Items (Blog/Articles)
For structured content like blog posts, we use a separate model:

```python
class ContentItem(db.Model):
    id = db.Column(db.Integer, primary_key=True)
    type = db.Column(db.String(50), nullable=False)        # 'blog', 'news', 'guide'
    title = db.Column(db.String(200), nullable=False)
    slug = db.Column(db.String(250), unique=True, nullable=False)
    summary = db.Column(db.Text)
    body = db.Column(db.Text)                               # Rich markdown/HTML
    hero_image_url = db.Column(db.String(500))
    tags = db.Column(db.JSON, default=list)
    status = db.Column(db.String(20), default='draft')      # draft, published
    published_at = db.Column(db.DateTime)
```

### Media Library
```python
class MediaItem(db.Model):
    id = db.Column(db.Integer, primary_key=True)
